Notes on the two non-obvious calls:

- **Unit 2**: every candidate that splits inside the backtick code (`` `g :=` `` / `` `x :=` ``) mangles literal code and was rejected outright. Of the rest, only c10 resolves the fused sentence boundary without severing the long first sentence at a non-boundary comma (the `, as in` phrase isn't an independent clause, so splitting there would leave a fragment) — the long first sentence is left intact as an advisory.
- **Unit 8**: the checker explicitly flags `_Phase 3_.` and `Use some tools...` as fused sentences, but no candidate ever isolates `_Phase 3_.` onto its own line — every one keeps it glued to the following sentence and only varies where it breaks *inside* that sentence. Since the reported fused pair is never actually resolved by any candidate, I flagged this as missing with the repair that does resolve it, following the same one-sentence-per-line treatment used for the `Derived types.` label in Unit 1.
